prompt:
Draft a professional, point-by-point rebuttal (response-to-reviewers) letter addressing every comment in the uploaded document. Open with a brief thank-you to the editor and reviewers. Then, for each comment, in order and clearly numbered:

Work in this order:
1. restate the reviewer's concern verbatim or faithfully paraphrased;
2. give a respectful, substantive response that either agrees and describes the fix or politely justifies the original choice with evidence;
3. specify the exact change made and where (section, line, or figure), quoting revised text where helpful. Base every response only on the manuscript and provided materials; never invent new results or citations, and flag with [VERIFY] anything needing confirmation from the authors. Keep the tone courteous and confident, never defensive. If a reviewer comment is unclear or requires a decision only the authors can make, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise note the assumption. Save the letter as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize key responses in chat.

Write a structured abstract for the uploaded manuscript, or from these details: {details}. Cover:

Work in this order:
1. Background/objective (1-2 sentences);
2. Methods (2-3 sentences);
3. Results (2-3 sentences with the key quantitative findings and statistics);
4. Conclusions (1-2 sentences stating significance and implications). Keep it within {word_limit} words. Use only information present in the source material; never fabricate results, numbers, or citations, and flag with [VERIFY] anything you cannot confirm. Ensure the abstract is self-contained, conveys the single key message, avoids undefined abbreviations, and matches the manuscript's claims without overstating them. If the key findings or word limit are missing or ambiguous in a way that changes the abstract, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumption. Save the abstract as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and paste the final text into chat with the word count.

Write a concise, professional cover letter (roughly one page) for submitting the uploaded manuscript to {journal}, addressed to the Editor-in-Chief. Include:

Work in this order:
1. the manuscript title and a brief summary of the study;
2. the key findings and why they matter;
3. a specific, non-generic statement of why the journal and its readership are the right fit;
4. a statement of originality, that the work is not under concurrent consideration elsewhere, and that all authors approved the submission;
5. suggested (and, if relevant, opposed) reviewers. Draw all claims from the manuscript; never overstate findings or invent details, and flag with [VERIFY] anything you cannot confirm (e.g., author names, corresponding-author contact). If essential details are missing or ambiguous, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise note placeholders. Save the cover letter as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Based on the uploaded protocol, data, or experimental notes, write a detailed Methods section suitable for a journal manuscript, written in past tense and the conventional impersonal scientific style. Cover, with clear subheadings:

Work in this order:
1. Study design and setting;
2. Materials, reagents, equipment, and where applicable catalog numbers, software, and versions;
3. Experimental procedures in enough operational detail for an independent lab to reproduce them;
4. Data collection methods and outcome measures;
5. Statistical analysis approach, including tests, assumptions, software, significance threshold, and handling of missing data. Use only details present in the uploaded material; never invent parameters, reagents, or values, and flag any gap with [DETAIL NEEDED] rather than guessing. Note ethics/approval and reporting-standard requirements where relevant. If a missing detail would materially affect reproducibility, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise list the gaps. Save the Methods section as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Write a Results section from the uploaded data, figures, and statistical outputs, reporting findings objectively in logical order with no interpretation, mechanism, or comparison to other work. For each result:

Work in this order:
1. state the finding clearly in plain scientific prose;
2. reference the relevant figure or table by number;
3. report the exact statistics present in the source (test statistic, degrees of freedom, p-value, confidence interval, and effect size). {focus} Use only values that appear in the uploaded material; never fabricate or round away statistics or infer numbers that are not provided, and flag any missing or ambiguous value with [VERIFY] rather than guessing. Keep tense consistent and define abbreviations on first use. If a statistical output is unclear in a way that changes how a result is reported, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise note the gap. Save the Results section (with figure/table references) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Write a Discussion section for the uploaded manuscript or these results: {findings}. Structure it as:

Work in this order:
1. restate the main findings and their direct answer to the research question, without re-reporting every statistic;
2. interpret the findings in the context of existing literature, noting agreement and conflict with prior work;
3. discuss plausible mechanisms and the practical or theoretical implications;
4. candidly acknowledge limitations and their effect on interpretation;
5. suggest concrete future directions. Search the literature for real, verifiable sources to support every interpretive claim; never fabricate citations or overstate causation beyond what the data support, and flag with [VERIFY] anything you cannot confirm. If the findings provided are too sparse or ambiguous to interpret responsibly, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the Discussion section (with references) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Write an Introduction section for a paper on: {topic}, using the classic funnel structure:

Work in this order:
1. broad context and significance of the field;
2. a focused review of key prior work, citing real, verifiable papers;
3. a clear statement of the gap or unanswered question;
4. the objective, hypothesis, and approach of the current study;
5. a brief signpost of what follows. Search the literature so that every citation is accurate (author, year, title); never fabricate references or attribute claims to the wrong source, and flag with [VERIFY] anything you cannot confirm. Build a logical argument that motivates the study rather than listing papers, and avoid overstating novelty. If the scope or intended contribution is ambiguous in a way that changes the framing, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the Introduction (with references) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Prepare supplementary materials for the uploaded manuscript, derived strictly from its content and data. Include, as relevant:

Work in this order:
1. detailed methods and parameters omitted from the main text for length;
2. supplementary figures, each with a self-contained caption;
3. supplementary tables with clear headers and units;
4. additional or sensitivity statistical analyses;
5. raw or summarized data tables. Number every item consistently (Fig. S1, Table S1, Supplementary Note 1, etc.), ensure each is referenced from the main text, and keep notation and units consistent with the main manuscript. Use only information present in the uploaded material; never fabricate data, figures, or statistics, and flag any gap with [DATA NEEDED] rather than inventing content. If a needed input is missing or ambiguous, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise note the gap. Save the supplementary document (with tables and figure captions) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize the items prepared in chat.

Audit the references in the uploaded manuscript and produce {style}-formatted output.

Work in this order:
1. Verify each cited work actually exists and is correctly attributed (authors, year, title, venue), using web/literature search, and flag any reference you cannot confirm rather than assuming it is valid;
2. fix formatting inconsistencies and bring both in-text citations and the reference list into a consistent style;
3. identify text claims that need a citation but lack one;
4. suggest more recent, authoritative, or directly relevant references where appropriate, clearly marked as suggestions;
5. note duplicate, retracted, or broken entries. Never invent citations, DOIs, or bibliographic details; mark anything unverified as [VERIFY]. If the citation style is unspecified or the manuscript mixes styles in a way that changes the fix, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state the style you applied. Save a report (corrected reference list plus a list of issues and suggested citations) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize the key findings in chat.

Draft a one-page Specific Aims page for a grant proposal on: {topic}, targeting {agency} and following that mechanism's conventions and length. Structure it as:

Work in this order:
1. an opening paragraph establishing significance and the critical knowledge gap;
2. a statement of the long-term goal, the overall objective of this proposal, and the central hypothesis with its basis;
3. 2-3 specific aims, each with a brief rationale, the approach, and the expected outcome, written as testable objectives that are related but not mutually dependent;
4. a closing paragraph on expected impact and how it advances the field. Search the literature to ground motivating claims in real, verifiable sources; never fabricate citations, statistics, or preliminary data, and flag with [VERIFY] anything unconfirmed. If the hypothesis or scope is ambiguous in a way that changes the aims, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the Specific Aims page (with references) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Create a detailed research plan for investigating: {topic}. Include:

Work in this order:
1. Background and rationale grounded in prior work;
2. clear, testable hypotheses;
3. experimental design and methods sufficient to judge feasibility and rigor, including key variables, controls, sample-size/power considerations, and analysis approach;
4. expected outcomes plus interpretation and concrete alternative strategies if results differ;
5. a timeline with milestones and go/no-go decision points;
6. required resources and personnel. Where you cite evidence, use real, verifiable sources and never fabricate citations, preliminary data, or statistics; flag anything needing a real reference with [VERIFY]. Note the main scientific and feasibility risks and caveats. If the research question or scope is ambiguous in a way that materially changes the plan, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions explicitly. Save the research plan as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and give a brief summary in chat.

Draft a biosketch for {name} for a {agency} proposal on {topic}, following the current {agency} biosketch format, structure, and length limits. Include:

Work in this order:
1. a Personal Statement connecting the candidate's expertise and prior work directly to this project;
2. Positions, Scientific Appointments, and Honors in reverse-chronological order;
3. Contributions to Science (up to 5, each a short narrative with up to 4 representative publications);
4. Research Support, current and completed, if applicable. Use only information the user provides or that is publicly verifiable; never fabricate degrees, positions, honors, publications, or grant numbers, and clearly mark with [PROVIDE] or [VERIFY] any field you cannot fill from given material. Write in a confident, specific tone that frames the candidate as uniquely suited to the work. If essential biographical details are missing, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise leave clearly-labeled placeholders. Save the biosketch as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Write the Significance section for a grant proposal on: {topic}. Build a persuasive, evidence-based case that:

Work in this order:
1. establishes the scope, burden, or importance of the problem;
2. reviews what is currently known and pinpoints the critical gap or barrier the field faces;
3. explains how the proposed research will fill that gap and why it is timely;
4. describes the expected impact on scientific knowledge, methods, or practice if the work succeeds. Search the literature and cite real, verifiable statistics and prior findings to motivate the work; never fabricate citations or numbers, and flag with [VERIFY] anything you cannot confirm. Write persuasively without overstating certainty, and keep the focus on the gap and payoff. If the scope or intended impact is ambiguous in a way that changes the framing, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the Significance section (with references) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Write the Innovation section for a grant proposal on: {topic}. Make a specific, defensible case for novelty by describing:

Work in this order:
1. how the proposed approach departs from and improves on current practice or the status quo;
2. the new technologies, methods, models, or reagents being developed or applied;
3. the novel conceptual framework, hypothesis, or perspective introduced;
4. why this innovation matters for advancing the field and shifting current paradigms. Be concrete and contrast with existing methods rather than making generic claims like 'first-ever'; where you assert that something is new or better, ground it in real, verifiable literature and flag with [VERIFY] anything unconfirmed. Never fabricate citations or prior-art comparisons. If the distinctive approach is ambiguous in a way that changes the argument, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the Innovation section (with references) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Write the Approach/Research Strategy section for a grant on: {topic}, formatted for {agency} conventions and length. For each specific aim, cover:

Work in this order:
1. rationale and the hypothesis being tested;
2. preliminary data supporting feasibility (clearly labeled, using only data the user provides; never fabricate results or figures);
3. detailed experimental design including key variables, controls, sample size/power, and rigor and reproducibility measures;
4. expected results and how they will be interpreted;
5. potential pitfalls with concrete alternative approaches;
6. a timeline with milestones. Ensure aims are logically connected but not fatally dependent on one another. Cite real, verifiable sources for any external claim and flag unconfirmed items with [VERIFY]. If aims, design, or available preliminary data are ambiguous in a way that materially changes the strategy, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the section (with references) as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Create a detailed project timeline for a {duration} project on: {topic}. Include:

Work in this order:
1. a Gantt chart laying out all tasks grouped by aim across the full duration;
2. milestones and deliverables with target dates;
3. go/no-go decision points;
4. personnel assignments per task;
5. dependencies and critical-path relationships between tasks. Render the Gantt chart as an actual figure (e.g., with matplotlib), use realistic, clearly-stated task durations, and note key assumptions and scheduling risks. Base tasks on the proposed work rather than inventing unrelated activities, and flag anything that needs the user's input with [CONFIRM]. If the aims or scope are too vague to schedule meaningfully, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the Gantt chart image plus a written timeline narrative (milestones, decision points, dependencies) together as well-named files in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize the plan briefly in chat.

Write a Data Management Plan (or Data Management and Sharing Plan) for a {agency} grant on: {topic}, following the current {agency} policy and required format and length. Cover:

Work in this order:
1. the types, formats, and estimated volume of data generated;
2. data and metadata standards;
3. access, sharing, and reuse policies, including the repository and timeline for sharing;
4. archiving and long-term preservation;
5. privacy, security, consent, and any human-subjects or proprietary considerations;
6. roles, responsibilities, and oversight. Look up the relevant policy and state the version you relied on; never invent repository names, identifiers, or policy clauses, and flag with [VERIFY] anything you cannot confirm. Note any compliance gaps or caveats. If essential details (data types or sharing constraints) are missing or ambiguous, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the plan as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Write a Broader Impacts section (NSF) or Dissemination Plan for a proposal on: {topic}, following the relevant funder's review criteria. Include concrete, well-justified activities for:

Work in this order:
1. education and training (students, mentees, curriculum);
2. broadening participation of groups underrepresented in STEM, with specific recruitment and support mechanisms;
3. public engagement and outreach;
4. potential societal, economic, or health benefits tied to the science;
5. dissemination through open-access publication, data and code sharing, and community resources. Propose realistic, measurable activities with assessment metrics rather than vague aspirations, and tie them to the project's resources and setting. Do not fabricate partnerships, programs, or statistics; flag anything the applicant must confirm with [VERIFY]. If key context (institution programs or target audiences) is missing in a way that changes the plan, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the section as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Create a risk mitigation plan for a research project on: {topic}. For each aim:

Work in this order:
1. identify the key scientific, technical, and operational risks;
2. rate each by likelihood and impact (e.g., low/medium/high) with a brief rationale;
3. describe specific mitigation strategies and credible alternative approaches that keep the project on track;
4. define early warning indicators and the decision/trigger point for switching to a fallback;
5. summarize everything in a risk matrix visualization (likelihood vs. impact) rendered as an actual figure. Base risks on the real demands of the proposed work rather than generic boilerplate, and avoid overstating or understating severity. Where a risk rests on an external assumption, state it and flag unverified claims with [VERIFY]. If the aims or scope are too vague to assess risk meaningfully, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state your assumptions. Save the risk matrix image and a written risk register together as well-named files in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize the top risks in chat.

Write a Facilities, Equipment, and Other Resources section for a grant on: {topic} at {institution}, following the conventional funder format (descriptive narrative, no direct cost figures). Describe, with subheadings:

Work in this order:
1. laboratory space and major equipment relevant to the aims;
2. computational resources (clusters, storage, software);
3. core facilities and shared instrumentation;
4. animal, clinical, or human-subjects facilities if applicable;
5. institutional support, collaborations, and intellectual environment;
6. office and meeting space. Frame each resource around how it enables the proposed research. Use only resources the user provides or that are publicly verifiable for that institution; never invent instruments, core facilities, or capabilities, and clearly mark anything to be confirmed with [VERIFY] or [PROVIDE]. If key facility details are missing in a way that materially affects the section, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise leave labeled placeholders. Save the section as a well-named Markdown file in the sandbox for preview and download, and summarize it briefly in chat.

Analyze the uploaded survey data (n={n} respondents). If the questionnaire structure, scale coding, or weighting variable is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Report response/completion rates and assess non-response and coverage bias against the target population;
2. Descriptive statistics for all items, flagging missingness and its mechanism;
3. Cross-tabulations with chi-square (report Cramer's V) for key comparisons, applying multiple-comparison correction;
4. Likert analysis: means, medians, distributions, treating ordinal scales appropriately;
5. Factor analysis or PCA for scale items, reporting reliability (Cronbach's alpha);
6. Regression models predicting {outcome} with effect sizes and 95% CIs, applying survey weights if provided. Use careful causal language. Set a random seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note sampling limitations.

Perform text mining on the uploaded corpus ({corpus_type}). If language, time field, or unit of analysis is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Preprocess: tokenize, normalize case, remove stopwords, lemmatize, and document filtering decisions;
2. Generate word-frequency and TF-IDF analysis;
3. Run LDA topic modeling with {n_topics} topics (default 10), reporting coherence to justify topic count;
4. Perform named entity recognition;
5. Analyze sentiment distribution with a validated model, noting its limits;
6. Visualize word clouds, topic networks, and temporal trends. Be careful that frequencies reflect the corpus, not a representative population; avoid overgeneralizing. Set a random seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, topic/term tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key themes in chat, name the files, and note corpus and model limitations.

Perform a demographic analysis for: {population}. If the geographic level, age bands, or data vintage is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Compile population data (age, sex, ethnicity, education, income) from real, cited sources (e.g., census/UN/national statistics), recording vintage and definitions;
2. Create population pyramids and distribution charts;
3. Analyze trends over {period} with appropriate rate standardization;
4. Compute dependency ratios and aging indicators;
5. Compare with national/regional benchmarks;
6. Project future demographics using the cohort-component method, stating fertility/mortality/migration assumptions and showing uncertainty bands across scenarios. Do not fabricate figures; cite each source. Record package versions and set seeds for any stochastic projections. Save figures as PNG, projection tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note data and assumption limitations.

Analyze the uploaded social network data. If directedness, edge weighting, or node identity is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Compute network statistics (size, density, diameter, average path length, clustering coefficient), specifying directed vs undirected;
2. Calculate centrality measures (degree, betweenness, closeness, eigenvector);
3. Detect communities (Louvain), reporting modularity and stability across runs;
4. Identify bridges and brokers (e.g., high betweenness, structural holes);
5. Analyze information-flow patterns;
6. Visualize with community coloring and centrality-based node sizing. Compare key metrics against a null model (configuration/random graph) to gauge significance. Set a random seed for stochastic algorithms and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, node/edge metric tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key findings in chat, name the files, and note boundary-specification and sampling limitations.

Analyze sentiment in the uploaded social media data about: {topic}. If platform, language, or the date field is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Classify posts as positive/negative/neutral using a validated NLP model, reporting accuracy/F1 against any labeled subset;
2. Track sentiment over time with confidence bands;
3. Identify key themes driving positive and negative sentiment;
4. Break down sentiment by demographic or geographic group if available, with effect sizes and CIs;
5. Detect bot or inauthentic activity and assess its impact;
6. Generate a sentiment report with actionable, caveated insights. Stress that the sample is not a representative population; avoid causal claims. Set a random seed and record package/model versions. Save figures as PNG, classified-data and summary tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note sampling and model limitations.

Evaluate the causal impact of {policy} on {outcome} using {method}. If treatment timing, the comparison group, or the running/instrument variable is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions; cite real data sources and do not fabricate.

Work in this order:
1. Define treatment and control groups and the identifying assumptions for the chosen method;
2. Apply it with appropriate, clustered standard errors;
3. Test the key identifying assumption (parallel trends for DiD, continuity/bandwidth for RDD, balance for PSM, instrument relevance/exclusion for IV);
4. Estimate the treatment effect with 95% confidence intervals and effect size;
5. Run robustness checks (placebo/falsification tests, alternative specifications, sensitivity to bandwidth/matching);
6. Interpret findings with careful causal language and discuss policy implications. Set a seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, estimate tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note threats to validity.

Analyze education outcomes from the uploaded data. If outcome definition, grouping, or available covariates are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Describe student demographics and performance distributions, flagging missingness;
2. Compare results between {groups} with effect sizes, 95% CIs, and multiple-comparison correction;
3. Analyze achievement gaps by subgroup;
4. Build a predictive model for {outcome} with proper train/test splits and cross-validation, reporting calibrated performance and key predictors while distinguishing association from causation;
5. Evaluate the effect of {intervention} if intervention data are available, controlling for SES and prior achievement and accounting for clustering within schools/classes;
6. Summarize practical implications. Set a random seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, result tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note confounding and generalizability limitations.

Analyze public opinion on {topic} from the uploaded polling data. If the weighting targets, question wording, or survey mode are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions; cite real comparison polls and do not fabricate figures.

Work in this order:
1. Weight responses to known population margins for representativeness, documenting the scheme;
2. Calculate point estimates with design-based margins of error;
3. Analyze trends over time, distinguishing real change from sampling noise;
4. Break down by demographics (age, gender, education, party, region) with CIs and multiple-comparison correction;
5. Identify opinion drivers via weighted regression, using careful (non-causal) language;
6. Compare with historical polls and flag significant shifts versus house/mode effects. Set a seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, estimate tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note coverage and nonresponse limitations.

Analyze migration patterns for {region}. If the spatial unit, time window, or flow definition is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions; cite real public sources (e.g., UN DESA, national statistics) and do not fabricate.

Work in this order:
1. Map origin-destination flows from the uploaded or public data, noting definitions and coverage;
2. Quantify net migration rates per 1,000 with consistent denominators;
3. Analyze push and pull factors;
4. Model determinants with a gravity model (log-linear/Poisson PPML), reporting coefficients with 95% CIs and goodness of fit;
5. Assess demographic impact on sending and receiving regions;
6. Visualize flows on maps and chord diagrams. Use careful causal language and check robustness to specification. Set a seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, flow and model tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note data and measurement limitations.

Measure inequality in {dimension} using the uploaded data. If the income/wealth concept, equivalence scale, or weighting is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions; cite real benchmarks and do not fabricate.

Work in this order:
1. Calculate the Gini coefficient (with bootstrap CIs) and plot the Lorenz curve, applying sample weights;
2. Compute percentile ratios (90/10, 80/20) and top/bottom shares;
3. Decompose inequality by {subgroups} (within vs between);
4. Analyze trends over time, adjusting for inflation/currency where relevant;
5. Compare with international benchmarks;
6. Identify key drivers using decomposition methods (Blinder-Oaxaca, Theil), reporting contributions with uncertainty. Set a seed for bootstrap/resampling and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, index and decomposition tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note definition-sensitivity and top-coding limitations.

Analyze data from the uploaded behavioral experiment ({design}). If the dependent variable, condition coding, or repeated-measures structure is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Check randomization balance across conditions;
2. Analyze manipulation checks;
3. Test main hypotheses with {analysis_type}, verifying its assumptions (normality, sphericity, homogeneity) and using robust alternatives if violated;
4. Analyze moderators and mediators with appropriate models;
5. Report effect sizes (Cohen's d, partial eta-squared) with 95% CIs and correct for multiple comparisons;
6. Check for demand, order, and attrition effects;
7. Produce APA-formatted results with publication-quality figures. Do not report observed post-hoc power; report minimum detectable effects or a sensitivity analysis at the achieved sample size, and use careful causal language. Set a random seed and record package versions. Save figures as PNG, result tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key results in chat, name the files, and note limitations.

Perform content analysis on the uploaded documents/media. If the unit of analysis, category definitions, or language is ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Develop an explicit codebook for {categories} with definitions and decision rules;
2. Apply automated coding via NLP and validate against a hand-coded subset;
3. Calculate inter-rater reliability (Cohen's kappa, or Krippendorff's alpha for multiple coders) and report agreement;
4. Analyze code frequency and co-occurrence with effect sizes;
5. Compare content across {groups} or time periods using appropriate tests with multiple-comparison correction;
6. Identify dominant frames and narratives, distinguishing prevalence from interpretation. Set a random seed and record package/model versions. Save figures as PNG, coded-data and frequency tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize key findings in chat, name the files, and note coding-validity and corpus limitations.

Set up and run a CFD simulation for: {geometry}. If geometry dimensions, fluid properties, or flow regime are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Define the geometry, characteristic length, and meshing strategy, computing the Reynolds number to justify the regime;
2. Set boundary conditions: {conditions}, using consistent SI units;
3. State the governing equations (continuity, Navier-Stokes), assumptions (incompressible/steady, etc.), and select a turbulence model (k-epsilon, k-omega SST, or LES) with rationale and y+ targets;
4. Run to convergence, monitoring scaled residuals and key quantities;
5. Verify mesh-independence via grid refinement (report GCI/discretization error) and check numerical stability (CFL);
6. Validate against an analytic case or published benchmark, reporting error. Post-process velocity/pressure fields and drag/lift coefficients, with streamlines and contours. Record solver settings and package versions. Save figures as PNG, coefficient/convergence tables as CSV, and a Markdown findings report to the sandbox; summarize results in chat, name the files, and note modeling limitations.

Before expensive execution, inspect available local resources and required binaries. If the full workload exceeds local capacity, use configured Modal compute only when authorized; otherwise run a bounded pilot or produce a validated execution plan. Never imply that an unexecuted full run completed.

Process the uploaded signal data. If sample rate, channel layout, or units are ambiguous and would change results, ask the minimum set of blocking questions together via the interview tool; otherwise state assumptions.

Work in this order:
1. Characterize the signal (sample rate, duration, channels, units) and verify the Nyquist criterion;
2. Apply filtering: {filter_spec}, reporting filter type/order, using zero-phase filtering where phase matters, and showing the frequency response;
3. Compute FFT and power spectral density with appropriate windowing;
4. Perform time-frequency analysis (STFT, wavelet transform);
